> which makes me believe its not the data but the code

Well, your group sizes are between 19 and 69, so I think that could be a pretty big problem, especially for categorical indicators.  Simulations have shown more convergence problems (and less reliably estimates and tests) at smaller N with DWLS estimation.

> However: writing measurement invariance out in full is successful e.g.
> Config    <- cfa(Model, data = Df, missing = "pairwise", ordered
>                = c("Q15_5", "Q16_5", "Q17_5", "Q18_5",
>                    "Q19_5", "Q20_5", "Q21_5", "Q22_5", "Q23_5",
>                    "Q24_5", "Q25_5", "Q26_5", "Q27_5", "Q28_5"), group = "adaptations")
> etc.
>
> comp <- compareFit(Config, Weak, Strong, Strict, nested = TRUE)
> summary(comp).....

In that case, I'm guessing the problem is the Millsap & Yun-Tein (2004) parameterization implemented in measurementInvarianceCat().  You are not the first to notice that this model fails to converge, when a statistically equivalent model (the one you specified in Config) does converge.  I even noticed this with simulated data at much larger samples than you had.  Since you had no problem fitting the model you specified yourself, I think you already found the best solution.

